{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וסמך", :translit "v'samach", :gloss "and he leaned"}], :mechanisms [], :frame nil} {:quotes [{:he "אשר לא יקים", :translit "asher lo yakim", :gloss "whoever does not uphold"} {:he "להכות נפש", :translit "lehakot nefesh", :gloss "to strike a soul"}], :mechanions [{:kind "other", :note "Reading להכות נפש ('to strike a soul') as a pointer to the bloodshed equation."}], :frame {:text "The tradition that one who sheds blood is considered as if he transgressed the entire Torah appears in multiple sources. The Sages state that a murderer is likened to one who diminished the divine likeness, as Genesis 9:6 establishes that man is created in the image of God. The further midrashic equation that murder equals transgressing the whole Torah is rooted in the principle that one who violates a single commandment is regarded as though he violated all of them (Jerusalem Talmud, Pe'ah 1:1 / Shevu'ot 4:2), applied paradigmatically to bloodshed.", :sources ["Genesis 9:6" "Jerusalem Talmud Pe'ah 1:1" "Jerusalem Talmud Shevu'ot 4:2"]}, :mechanisms []} {:quotes [{:he "אמן", :translit "amen", :gloss "amen"} {:he "הוי' אדנ\"י", :translit " Havayah Adonai", :gloss "Y-H-V-H, my Lord"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "אמן", :b "הוי' אדנ\"י"} {:kind :other, :note "Since Amen equals two divine Names, answering Amen carries the spiritual weight of uttering two Names, exceeding the single Name invoked by the one who blesses."}], :frame {:text "The Sages taught that answering Amen is greater than reciting the blessing itself, stated in Tractate Berakhot 53b. The basic principle appears in the Mishnah: within the Temple they would not answer 'Amen' but rather 'Blessed be His great Name forever and ever' (Berakhot 10:5 / Yoma 3:9). The idea that the divine Name requires reverent handling, and that answering Amen constitutes an act of affirming the divine Name, is rooted in Numbers 5:22, where Amen seals the oath.", :sources ["Berakhot 53b" "Berakhot 10:5" "Yoma 3:9" "Numbers 5:22"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :gematria, :a "אמן", :a-value 91, :b "הוי' אדנ\"י", :b-value 86, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :other, :note "Since Amen equals two divine Names, answering Amen carries the spiritual weight of uttering two Names, exceeding the single Name invoked by the one who blesses.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:39:57.462923977Z", :model "glm-5.2"}